    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the exchange around The Homeless line by line and unpack how the Russian works.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Все, я готов.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'All right, I am ready.' Kostya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'ready'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Пойдем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's go.' Kostya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'let's go'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Все, давайте.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'All right, see you.' Max leads with the direct command form meaning 'let's' to make the point directly in conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет бомжам.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Say hi to the bums.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'hi' directly with 'homeless person', combining 'hi' with 'homeless person'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Не бомжам, а бездомным.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not bums, but homeless.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'homeless person' directly with 'homeless person', combining 'homeless person' with 'homeless person'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Смотрите.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Look.' Max leads with the direct command form meaning 'look' to make the point directly in conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Заразу не принесите в дом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not bring infection home.' Max leads with the direct command form meaning 'bring', pairing it with 'infection' and 'building'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "От кого?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'From whom?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'of' directly with 'whom', combining 'of' with 'whom'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "От бездомных.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'From the homeless.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'homeless person'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "Мы будем осторожны.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We will be careful.' Kostya chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'will' for the ongoing action, followed by 'cautious'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надеюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'hope so'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anton",
      "t": "До вечера.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Until evening.' Kostya delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'evening'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Пока.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Bye.' Max delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'bye'. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
